< Forcing 768 tokens raises $q$ by 0.11--0.18 (all intervals exclude zero after a Bonferroni correction), almost all of it above 256 tokens, and recovers 42--85\% of the calculator's lead (Table~\ref{tab:main}). Re-scoring the same choices under a lognormal fit to the held-out prices gives 44--85\%, and under held-out prices shaded by 15\%, as rivals in a first-price market would bid, 34--83\%. The unforced run mostly measures more tokens, not natural stopping: 46--87\% of its traces reach the 1{,}024 cap for five models, while Granite-8B always stops, by 669 tokens in 90\% of traces. Dollar recovery is lower for five models because Cartier dominates the dollar gap (\$113--\$186 per decision at budget zero, \$2--\$15 elsewhere) and recovers least. Majority votes (ties broken by summed option probability) of two 384-token or five 128-token samples score 0.05--0.19 below one 768-token sample.
