< \paragraph{Most of the loss is after the computation.}
< Table~\ref{tab:traces} splits the 768-token gap using the trace's own written values. Arithmetic is 22\% of Granite-8B's gap and at most 5\% for the others. Options never reached account for 2--34\%, most for Qwen3-8B. In the remaining 62--93\% the trace had written the best option's value as the largest and the answer took another bid: in unfinished lists for the Qwen models (39--63\%), in finished ones for Phi-mini (82\%) and Granite-8B (61\%), and about equally for DeepSeek-8B. A full comparison takes a median of 440--656 tokens with five options and 544--840 with seven (Figure~\ref{fig:main}b). Within the same model and scenario, with budget fixed effects, a complete trace scores 0.075 [0.062, 0.088] higher than an unfinished one; this is a contrast, not the causal effect of finishing.
