< Taking the option with the largest value the trace wrote, once two values exist, recovers 73--96\% of the gap at 768 tokens and 65--88\% at 512, where the answer recovers 17--56\%; it never lowers $q$ by more than 0.026 at any budget and changes by at most 4 points for segment windows of 300--1{,}000 characters (Table~\ref{tab:main}, Figure~\ref{fig:main}c). The oracle reader falls below the answer at 256 tokens, where it acts on partial lists. On the synthetic traces, which we did not use to design the reader, it helps Phi-mini and Granite-8B (47\% to 86\%, 65\% to 73\%) but lowers recovery by 3--14 points for the other four, whose answers already track their traces. It works better as an audit. Flagging decisions where the reader and the answer disagree catches 53--88\% of wrong answers and 54--99\% of the lost value, with false alarms on 7--24\% of correct answers; sending flagged decisions to the calculator recovers 84--100\% of the gap while calling it in 18--57\% of decisions. The audit needs only the trace, so it applies where a platform or user can read an agent's reasoning but not change its tools.
