> Taking the option with the largest value the trace wrote, once two values exist, recovers 73--96\% of the gap at 768 tokens and 65--88\% at 512 (intervals within $\pm$6 points), where the answer recovers 17--56\%; it never lowers $q$ by more than 0.026 at any budget and changes by at most 4 points for segment windows of 300--1{,}000 characters (Table~\ref{tab:main}, Figure~\ref{fig:main}c). The oracle reader falls below the answer at 256 tokens, where it acts on partial lists. As a replacement it does not transfer: on the synthetic traces, not used in its design, it helps Phi-mini and Granite-8B but lowers recovery by 3--14 points for the other four. It works better as an audit. Flagging decisions where the reader and the answer disagree catches 53--88\% of wrong answers and 54--99\% of the lost value, with false alarms on 7--24\% of correct answers; sending flagged decisions to the calculator recovers 84--100\% of the gap while calling it in 18--57\% of decisions. On the synthetic traces the same audit recovers 82--100\% (recall 51--91\%, false alarms 11--26\%). Calling the calculator every time recovers everything, so the audit is useful only where reasoning must stay in the loop, for example to check an agent whose tools cannot be changed, where flagging is the output and the platform's own calculation is the fallback.
